<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Social Contract</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1762">1762</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as On the Social Contract; or, Principles of Political Right, Du contrat social</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Social Contract (Du contrat social) is a 1762 book by the Genevan philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Jean-Jacques Rousseau</a> on how to establish legitimate authority in a political community, one compatible with individual freedom. It argues against the idea that monarchs were divinely empowered to legislate and holds that only the general will of the people has the right to legislate, for only then do the people obey only themselves and remain free. Rousseau separates the sovereign from the government, the executive power, and argues that equality is essential for the attainment of liberty. The book helped inspire political reforms or revolutions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, especially in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-social-contract.jpg" alt="The Social Contract" width="318" height="510" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Social_contract_rousseau_page.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…life, liberty and property and in the consent of the governed; Montesquieu urged the separation of legislative, executive and judicial power; and Jean-Jacques Rousseau, whose <b>The Social Contract</b> opens “Man is born free, and everywhere he is in chains”, placed sovereignty in the people and their general will.</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>…(1748) that it is natural for a republic to have only a small territory, and Jean-Jacques Rousseau, who signed himself a citizen of Geneva, held in <b>The Social Contract</b> (1762) that a people could make its own laws only in a state small enough for it to assemble.</blockquote><a href="/government/city-state">City-state · Strengths</a></li><li><blockquote>Jean-Jacques Rousseau, in <b>The Social Contract</b> (1762), accepted that in the gravest danger the laws might be suspended and a supreme ruler named, provided his term was short and could not be extended.</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · Strengths</a></li><li><blockquote>Jean-Jacques Rousseau, a citizen of the Republic of Geneva, argued in <b>The Social Contract</b> of 1762 that sovereignty could not be represented, so that a people that merely elected deputies was free only on election day.</blockquote><a href="/government/direct-democracy">Direct democracy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Rousseau's <b>The Social Contract</b> and Émile shaped political thought and education, and his Confessions helped create modern autobiography.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-28">28 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
